protection. Thus the part played by the oceanic feeling, which might 
seek something like the restoration of limitless narcissism, is ousted from 
a place in the foreground. The origin of the religious attitude can be 
traced back in clear outlines as far as the feeling of infantile helplessness. 
There may be something further behind that, but for the present it is 
wrapped in obscurity. 
I can imagine that the oceanic feeling became connected with religion 
later on. The 'oneness with the universe' which constitutes its ideational 
content sounds like a first attempt at a religious consolation, as though 
it were another way of disclaiming the danger which the ego recognizes 
as threatening it from the external world. Let me admit once more that 
it is very difficult for me to work with these almost intangible quantities. 
